spoint PROFILE root cause (gm-skill 'profile debug resolve all bottlenecks', 2026-06-18, branch dna-quality-fixes; reliable in-browser witness session 27, AMD Radeon iGPU, full-res ~1036x647). THE dominant frame bottleneck is NOT the forest and NOT the planet's GPU shading - it is the TWO `renderer.resetState()` calls bracketing the planet draw in client/core/TerrainBackdrop.js renderPlanet (lines 58 + 63). WITNESS (browser-18, override renderPlanet): noop=6.95ms(144fps); ONE resetState(no planet draw)=13.46ms; TWO resetState(no planet)=17.86ms; full base=15.8-17.9ms. browser-8 planet-no-op saved 9.63ms; browser-19 confirmed exactly 2 resetState/frame. So each renderer.resetState() costs ~5-6ms on this ANGLE/D3D driver and the pair = the entire ~9-11ms 'planet cost'; the planet's actual GPU draw (179 quads, 2 draw calls) is near-free. CONFOUND CORRECTED: octave count (uOctMax 12->4 via window.__octMax/etc), GRID (11->6 via __glGrid+clearCache), quad count (window.__splitFactor), aerial-march, water-pass (window.__waterSurface=false), and planet-only VDRS (window.__vdrs/__vdrsScale) ALL barely moved frame time (<=2ms) -> the fragment-shader-ALU hypothesis (PRD ff-planet-fragment-bound-rootcause) is WRONG/secondary; resetState is primary. A whole-canvas renderer.setSize(w/3,h/3) DID cut 17.25->6.93ms but that shrinks BOTH planet+scene fill and is not the right lever. FAILED FIX (reverted, do not repeat): replacing the LEADING resetState with a minimal manual unbind (bindVertexArray/useProgram/bindBuffer/bindFramebuffer null) -> mapspinner's drawElementsInstanced throws GL_INVALID_OPERATION(1282) + fps collapses; the leading resetState resolves more than VAO/program/buffer/fbo (likely texture-unit/sampler/transform-feedback state). SAFE RESOLUTION PATH (substantial, needs stable browser to land): cache the planet color+depth to an FBO and re-composite via a THREE fullscreen quad on static-camera frames (planet-orchestrator already caches the quad LIST but still redraws GPU + pays both resetStates every frame) -> eliminates the per-frame foreign-GL handoff; OR find a cheaper THREE<->foreign-GL state handoff than full resetState x2. CAVEAT: gm watcher restart kills the browser session (session 'not found' / chromium 'handle is closing' assertion); re-establish with a standalone `session new` before nav. ES-module HTTP cache survives about:blank -> force-fresh nav can still serve stale edited module.